Let's see this in action:

```java
Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student();
Student s3 = new Student();

s1.name = "Rahul";
s2.name = "Priya";
s3.name = "Arjun";
```

### What does this look like?

```text
       [ Student Class Blueprint ]
                   |
     +-------------+-------------+
     |             |             |
     v             v             v
[ Student s1 ]  [ Student s2 ]  [ Student s3 ]
name = "Rahul"  name = "Priya"  name = "Arjun"
```

### Interactive Predict-The-Output Challenge:
Look at this code carefully:

```java
Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student();

s1.name = "Rahul";
s2.name = "Priya";

// We change s1's name:
s1.name = "Vikram";

// What will this print?
System.out.println(s2.name);
```

**What does it print?**
It prints **`"Priya"`**!

Changing `s1.name` has zero effect on `s2.name`. Each object owns its own variables. This independence is what makes OOP so reliable when building software with thousands of users, orders, or products.

